{"pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis":{"id":"6774faed-7b29-4c14-8ee4-1749014a12b8","word":"pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"disease of the lungs","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pneumoultramicroscopicossilicovulcanoconiose"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Call it miner's asthma, silicosis, pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis, coal workers' pneumoconiosis, or black lung—they are all dust diseases with the same symptoms.","ref":"1980 March, Lorin E. Kerr, “Black Lung”, in Journal of Public Health Policy, volume 1, number 1, →JSTOR, page 50:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[35,80]],"id":"209f3235-72c8-4f04-aa0a-eebae9e92dae"},{"text":"I say that it must be the silica dust\nThat we breathed through our mouths and our noses\nThat brought pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis.","ref":"1998 August 27, Smokey, “Lament for a Lung Disease”, in talk.bizarre (Usenet), message-ID <6s3r8o$brt$1@camel15.mindspring.com>:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[101,146]],"id":"48de63c8-f96f-4269-84ca-ee407e9ebc3b"},{"text":"It's either pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis, or a bad cough.","ref":"2002 December 18, Pod, “Pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis”, in alt.fan.scarecrow (Usenet), message-ID <iHSL9.2091$h43.295898@stones>:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[12,57]],"id":"527d39d2-d931-45d0-bf0d-f742b9c9a6e0"},{"text":"I still can't watch House M.D. and not have my mind wonder[…] Even I can fear of^([sic]) having Pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis after watching it.","ref":"2011 April 28, Kurt D. Stradtman, Am I the Person My Mother Warned Me About?: A Four-year College Experience ... Only the Good Parts, Xlibris, →ISBN, →LCCN, page 90:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[96,141]],"id":"24952c1b-62ca-4044-bcd7-90d67c42fc9c"},{"text":"Regarding the lack of funding and attention do you agree that there should be parity of esteem between mental conditions such as body dysmorphia and physical conditions such as pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis.","ref":"2017 July 31, Michael Bryan, quotee, “Boy, 16, uses 'longest word' in Parliament”, in BBC News, archived from the original on 03 Aug 2017:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[177,222]],"id":"08662b98-a9e4-4d1c-abe4-a282d86f5b00"}],"categories":["English coinages","English compound terms","English entries referencing etymons with mismatched IDs","English entries with etymology trees","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nonce terms","English nouns","English terms coined by Everett Smith","English terms derived from the Proto-Indo-European root *h₂el- (other)","English terms derived from the Proto-Indo-European root *ken-","English terms derived from the Proto-Indo-European root *mey- (little)","English terms derived from the Proto-Indo-European root *plew-","English terms derived from the Proto-Indo-European root *speḱ-","English terms prefixed with pneumono-","English terms prefixed with silico-","English terms suffixed with -osis","English terms with quotations","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Long English words","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Pages with etymology trees","Rhymes:English/əʊsɪs","Rhymes:English/əʊsɪs/19 syllables","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Spanish translations","en:Diseases"],"links":[["disease","disease"],["microscopic","microscopic"],["silicate","silicate"],["particles","particle"],["eruption","eruption"],["volcano","volcano"]],"raw_glosses":["(nonce word) A disease of the lungs, allegedly caused by inhaling microscopic silicate particles originating from the eruption of a volcano."],"glosses":["A disease of the lungs, allegedly caused by inhaling microscopic silicate particles originating from the eruption of a volcano."],"tags":["nonce-word","uncountable"],"wikipedia":["National Puzzlers' League"],"id":"e5ae5cc6-39f3-4339-a696-1a106ae2171b"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"pneumoconiosis":{"id":"935f0506-f039-468e-9846-8425d97fe51c","word":"pneumoconiosis","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"disease of the lungs","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pneumoconiose"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Zhang Haichao was twenty-eight when he got pneumoconiosis. He was fit and well before he started working at an abrasive-materials factory in Xinmi, not far from Zhengzhou, the capital of Henan, where he comes from. On the job, he inhaled a huge amount of dust every day. In the second half of 2007, he began to cough and felt short of breath.","ref":"2012, Hsiao-Hung Pai, Scattered Sand: The Story of China's Rural Migrants, Verso Books, →ISBN, →LCCN, →OCLC, page 86:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[43,57]],"id":"b37c100c-3a08-4c10-95fe-8f8c3d612c73"},{"text":"Wu Dengfan, a 40-year-old farmer from Gulang county in Gansu who worked at a gold mine close to the Mongolian border from 1996 to 2006, had a similar experience.\n“We even preferred not to wear masks inside the mine, because it made breathing even more difficult,” said Wu, who has stage II pneumoconiosis. “When we finished our work and came out of the mine, each of us was completely covered in dust.”","ref":"2018 January 13, Alice Yan, “These migrant workers helped China prosper. Now they’re dying and not getting the help they need”, in South China Morning Post, →ISSN, →OCLC, archived from the original on 13 Jan 2018, People & Culture:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[290,304]],"id":"d3e7952c-5173-4086-9a16-3719e3521ceb"}],"topics":["medicine","pathology","sciences"],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English nouns with irregular plurals","English terms prefixed with pneumo-","English terms with quotations","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Mandarin terms with redundant transliterations","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Cantonese translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Esperanto translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Indonesian translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Latin translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Turkish translations","en:Diseases"],"links":[["pathology","pathology"],["disease","disease"],["lung","lung"]],"raw_glosses":["(pathology) A disease of the lungs caused by inhalation of particulate matter."],"glosses":["A disease of the lungs caused by inhalation of particulate matter."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"9b7dbda9-2856-492a-a571-be0077d32016"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"pneumatic":{"id":"bc79f3b1-0b78-47bb-937c-580db97808a5","word":"pneumatic","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"of or relating to air or other gases","tags":["masculine"],"word":"pneumático"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"of or relating to air or other gases","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pneumática"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"of or relating to pneumatics","tags":["masculine"],"word":"pneumático"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"of or relating to pneumatics","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pneumática"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"powered by, or filled with compressed air","tags":["masculine"],"word":"pneumático"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"powered by, or filled with compressed air","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pneumática"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["air","air"],["gas","gas"]],"glosses":["Of, relating to, or resembling air or other gases"],"id":"8085b1e2-9d00-45c5-a937-62137f6dcdaf"},{"links":[["pneumatics","pneumatics"]],"glosses":["Of or relating to pneumatics"],"id":"8b3157a2-476b-4bef-89ab-9d29919716b1"},{"examples":[{"text":"a pneumatic instrument or engine","bold_text_offsets":[[2,11]],"type":"example","id":"b7f3b7ce-33eb-4515-9d46-049acd2bfa72"}],"links":[["Powered","power"],["fill","fill"],["compressed air","compressed air"]],"glosses":["Powered by, or filled with, compressed air"],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"id":"e837f70b-b5ca-4dec-a00b-d48888e7b6fc"},{"examples":[{"text":"pneumatic cells or bones","bold_text_offsets":[[0,9]],"type":"example","id":"f38f5057-09b3-4263-9de7-c6d1b4258327"}],"topics":["biology","natural-sciences","zoology"],"categories":["English terms with usage examples","en:Zoology"],"links":[["zoology","zoology"],["cavities","cavities"]],"raw_glosses":["(zoology) Having cavities filled with air"],"glosses":["Having cavities filled with air"],"id":"43cc9d2c-cd9e-4ad3-98e9-6df7a03c6f33"},{"links":[["Spiritual","spiritual"],["pneuma","pneuma"]],"glosses":["Spiritual; of or relating to the pneuma"],"id":"b6fcc348-6110-4de0-b7e0-8b4ae561d42d"},{"examples":[{"text":"Her almost pneumatic bust tightened itself, and she was quite prepared to say uncharitable things, as she saw Felicia enter.","ref":"1899, Alan Dale, His Own Image: A Novel, G. W. Dillingham Company, page 59:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[11,20]],"id":"635ed390-e3b1-43c9-a615-399e221ea346"},{"text":"\"Every one says I'm awfully pneumatic,\" said Lenina reflectively, patting her own legs.","ref":"1932, Aldous Huxley, chapter 6, in Brave New World, London: Chatto & Windus:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[28,37]],"id":"9b9de868-267b-45fc-a6ab-4d40912f552c"},{"text":"This short but pneumatic woman had killed six men and yet for some reason he wasn't afraid.","ref":"2015, Mark Sanderson, Robin Hood Yard (Snow Hill 3), HarperCollins UK, page 255:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[15,24]],"id":"199dbe07-e8bc-4a1d-8779-c107f307e19e"}],"links":[["well-rounded","well-rounded"],["full","full"],["breasted","breasted"],["bouncy","bouncy"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"raw_glosses":["(of a woman) well-rounded; full-breasted; bouncy"],"glosses":["well-rounded; full-breasted; bouncy"],"raw_tags":["of a woman"],"id":"9c71257e-7897-4517-a6e7-cea7f992e816"}],"related":[{"word":"pneumaticity"},{"word":"pneumatization"}],"synonyms":[{"sense":"resembling air","word":"aereous"},{"word":"airy"},{"word":"gaseous"},{"sense":"spiritual","word":"pneumenous"},{"sense":"of a woman","word":"voluptuous"}]},"pneumonia":{"id":"b755679c-81c0-42da-bcd1-b85c61cb40d9","word":"pneumonia","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"inflammation of the lungs","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pneumonia"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"He had always been remarkably immune from such little ailments, and had only once in his life been ill, of a vicious pneumonia long ago at school.","ref":"1922, Michael Arlen, “2/9/1”, in “Piracy”: A Romantic Chronicle of These Days:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[117,126]],"id":"fb52728b-d02f-4933-93e0-b9f202029259"}],"links":[["virus","virus"],["bacteria","bacterium"],["microorganism","microorganism"]],"categories":["Cantonese terms with redundant transliterations","English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","E